  "Question": "\\(f(x)=x^2\\) and \\(y=f(2x)\\). What is the derivative of \\(y\\) at \\(x=1\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "8",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\((x^2)'\\) evaluated at \\(2x\\) is \\(2\\times(2x)\\), then multiply by \\(2\\). At \\(x=1\\), the result is \\(8\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Simplify the composition first\n\nSubstitute \\(2x\\) into the given formula for \\(f\\) before differentiating. Then \\(y=f(2x)=(2x)^2=4x^2\\), so the power and constant-multiple rules give \\(y'=8x\\). Hence \\(y'(1)=8\\).",
